      "text": "Let $a_n = \\sqrt{b_n}.$  Then $b_{n + 1} = (a_n)^2 b_{n + 1} = b_n^2.$  Since $a_1 = 1 + \\sqrt{7}$ and $a_2 = a_1 a_3,$ we have $b_1 = \\sqrt{7} > 0$ and $b_2^2 = b_1 \\cdot b_3^2.$  Furthermore, since $a_2 = a_1 a_3,$ we have $b_2 = b_1 = \\sqrt{7}.$\n\nThen $b_{n + 1} = b_n^2 \\ge 0,$ so $b_n$ is either always non-negative, or it eventually becomes negative.  But since $b_n > 0$ for all $n,$ $b_n$ eventually becomes positive.\n\nIn general, if $b_n > 0,$ then $b_{n + 1} = b_n^2 > 0.$  So eventually $b_n > 0.$  Thus, we must have $b_{1776} = b_2^8 = (\\sqrt{7})^8 = 7^4 = 2401 > 0$ because $b_{1776} = a_{1776} = a_{2009 - 233 \\cdot 4} = a_1.$\n\nLet $b_n = 7^{2k}$ for some integer $k.$  Then $b_1 = 7^2 = 49,$ $b_2 = 7^4 = 2401,$ $b_3 = 7^6.$\n\nAnother inspection shows $b_{1776} = 7^8,$ $b_{1777} = 7^{16},$ $b_{1778} = 7^{32},$ $b_{1779} = 7^{64},$ $b_{1780} = 7^{128},$ $b_{1781} = 7^{256},$ $b_{1782} = 7^{512},$ $b_{1783} = 7^{1024},$ $b_{1784} = 7^{2048},$ $b_{1785} = 7^{4096},$ $b_{1786} = 7^{8192},$ $b_{1787} = 7^{16384},$ and $b_{1788} = 7^{32768}.$\n\nSince $b_{1776} = 7^8$ and $b_{1778} = 7^{128},$ we also have $b_{1796} = 7^{256},$ $b_{1814} = 7^{512},$ $b_{1833} = 7^{1024},$ $b_{1851} = 7^{2048},$ $b_{1870} = 7^{4096},$ and so on.\n\nTo find a pattern, we notice that $b_{1776} = 7^8,$ $b_{1780} = 7^{128},$ $b_{1814} = 7^{512},$ $b_{1851} = 7^{1024},$ $b_{1909} = 7^{2048},$ and $b_{1967} = 7^{4096}.$\n\nSince $1796, 1814, 1909, 1967 \\% 13 = 5.$  Thus, $b_{1949} = 7^4$ and $b_{1967} = 7^4 = 2401 = a_{1776}.$  Therefore, $b_{2009} = 7^{48}.$\n\nIn summary, $b_{2009} = 7^8 = \\sqrt{(7^2)^4} = \\sqrt{7^8} = \\boxed{49}.$",
